.TH CVEC_DECLARE_SCOPED 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cvec_declare_scoped \- declare a vector variable destroyed automatically at scope exit
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cvector.h>

cvec_declare_scoped(v, type);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares a vector variable named v for elements of the given type, the
same way cvec_declare(3) does, and adds a cleanup handler that destroys
the vector when control leaves the surrounding scope. No vector is
created: v is initialized to NULL and stays NULL until cvec_init(3) or
cvec_init_mp(3) gives it a vector.

That NULL is what makes the cleanup handler safe, because the handler runs
on every exit from the scope, including one that happens before anything
has assigned the variable. If the variable were left indeterminate, an
early return between the declaration and the creation would hand an
arbitrary value to the handler. Both companion type variables also start
at NULL for the same reason, so nothing in the block reads an
indeterminate object.

The vector is destroyed on every path out of the block: the closing
brace, a return, a break, a continue or a goto out of the block. The
variable is set to NULL as the vector is destroyed, so an explicit
cvec_destroy(3) earlier in the block is safe and frees nothing twice.

When you can create the vector on the same line as the declaration, use
cvec_construct_scoped(3) or cvec_construct_mp_scoped(3) instead.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
v     Name of the vector variable to declare
type  Element type, for example int, double or struct foo
      Any type name works, a function pointer type such
      as void (*)(int) and an array type such as int[4]
      included. For an array element type, give
      cvec_push(3) an array or a string literal, whose
      bytes the macro copies into the element
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro is built on __attribute__((cleanup)), so it needs GCC or Clang.
On a toolchain without that attribute, use cvec_declare(3) with an
explicit cvec_destroy(3).

It expands to a sequence of declarations and needs a semicolon at the end
of the call, so it goes where declarations are legal and can't be the
body of an if or a loop without braces.

A block that leaves v at NULL is fine, because the cleanup handler does
nothing for a NULL vector.

If the vector holds pointers to heap buffers, the elements are not freed
one by one, so free the element data before leaving the scope.
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
The vector must not outlive its scope. Don't return v, and don't store it
anywhere that code reads after the end of the block. When ownership has
to leave the block, use cvec_declare(3).
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
void load(bool use_pool, ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *pool) {
    cvec_declare_scoped(vec, int);

    if (use_pool) {
        cvec_init_mp(vec, pool);
    } else {
        cvec_init(vec);
    }

    cvec_push(vec, 42);
}   /* vec is destroyed here, and on every earlier exit from the block */
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cvec_declare (3),
.BR cvec_construct_scoped (3),
.BR cvec_construct_mp_scoped (3),
.BR cvec_init (3),
.BR cvec_init_mp (3),
.BR cvec_destroy (3)
